+# External control helpers
+
+Optional global `encoder_helper` and `imaging_helper` settings select absolute
+executable paths. They are independent of the operating system's camera
+framework. The server invokes the executable directly, with one operation
+argument, a JSON request on stdin and a JSON response on stdout. It does not
+interpret command strings. Helpers may be compiled programs or executable
+scripts. Diagnostics belong on stderr.
+
+The request envelope contains `version: 1`, `operation` and an object `data`.
+The response contains `version: 1`, `status` and, on success, an object `data`.
+Status values are `ok`, `unsupported`, `not_found`, `invalid`, `busy`, `failed`.
+A protocol response, including an error status, uses process exit status zero.
+Non-zero exit, malformed output, unsupported protocol versions and output
+exceeding the CGI message size of 16 KiB are application failures. Return
+exactly one JSON object. Child output is drained while request input is sent.
+
+`helper_timeout_ms` is an optional positive deadline. Its default is zero,
+which leaves the deadline to the CGI host. Configure it below the HTTP
+server's script limit when helpers may block. The deadline uses uptime and
+includes both communication and child completion. Failed/timed-out children
+are terminated and reaped. Helpers must remain synchronous and must not
+daemonise or leave background work behind.
+
+Operation-specific encoder and imaging contracts are described alongside
+their service implementations. A helper is responsible for serialising
+changes to shared camera resources and for returning success only after a
+change is applied. The server does not commit its generated configuration.
